<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Świętopełk II, Duke of Pomerania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1195–<a href="/years/1266">1266</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Świętopełk II the Great · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samborides">Samborides</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Świętopełk II the Great (c. 1195–1266) was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, the most powerful of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samborides">Samborides</a>. In 1227 he threw off Polish overlordship and was involved in the killing of the Polish high duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-white">Leszek the White</a> at Gąsawa. He later fought long wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, at times in alliance with the pagan Prussians.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swietopelk-ii-duke-of-pomerania.jpg" alt="Świętopełk II, Duke of Pomerania" width="320" height="726" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herman Han (1574-1627) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swantopolk_II,_Duke_of_Pomerania.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Świętopełk was the son of Mestwin I, who had ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a> since about 1205 by appointment of the Polish high duke Władysław III Spindleshanks, and of his wife Swinisława. In 1216 or 1217 High Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-white">Leszek I the White</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> made him steward over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> territory, the largest of the four portions of Pomerelia. In 1218 he used a revolt of local knights against Danish rule to occupy the Lands of Schlawe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Stolp</a>. On his father's death he became guardian of his young brothers Sambor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Ratibor</a>, who later received Lubiszewo Tczewskie and Białogarda as their shares.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Exploiting the fragmentation of Piast Poland, Świętopełk promised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-odonic">Władysław Odonic</a> the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and Silesia in return for help against Leszek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-bearded">Henry the Bearded</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>. On 23 November 1227, at an assembly of Piast dukes at Gąsawa, Leszek was killed in an ambush set by Świętopełk and perhaps Władysław, and Henry was badly wounded. From 1227 he was the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samborides">Samborides</a> to style himself dux. In 1233–1234 he and Sambor joined a crusading army that refortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwidzyn">Kwidzyn</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and defeated the Pomesanians at the battle of the River Sorge.</p><p>In 1238 he conquered the Duchy of Pomerania-Schlawe, which brought war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a>. His brothers refused to support him, and the quarrel grew into a civil war; driven from their lands, they turned to their Piast relatives in Greater Poland and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>. Świętopełk, until then known as a supporter of the Church, allied with the pagan Prussians and played a key part in the First Prussian Uprising, which began in 1242.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The uprising failed, and a peace treaty mediated by a papal legate was signed on 24 November 1248. Świętopełk had to return his brothers' lands, let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> pass through his domains, stop charging tolls on ships on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and give no more aid to the Prussians. He kept these terms and stayed out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-prussian-uprising">Great Prussian Uprising</a> of 1260–1274. He died on 11 January 1266, and his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestwin-ii">Mestwin II</a> and Wartislaw II inherited his lands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Świętopełk II, Duke of Pomerania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Swietopelk II the Great</b>, who ruled from the 1220s to 1266, threw off Polish overlordship, took the ducal title and fought long wars against the Teutonic Order.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerelia">Duchy of Pomerelia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.35,18.65&amp;year=1241" aria-label="Gdańsk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4962 301 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Świętopełk II, Duke of Pomerania"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5412 571h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5412 571h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=54.35,18.65&amp;year=1241">What was here in 1241? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
